From: xnulinux
Subject: [PATCH] linux64: on an EL1 host too, a pending urgent preemption is taken when preemption is re-enabled

kernel_preempt_check is what _enable_preemption calls when the count reaches 0 with AST_URGENT pending: the place a
thread is preempted when the preemption was asked for while it had preemption off (it woke a thread that should run
instead while it held a spinlock; the hosted timer, run from where interrupts are re-enabled, expired its quantum).
The pure EL0 host gave the function a hosted branch on XNU's virtual interrupt flag, because DAIF cannot be read at
EL0, under `if (linux64_el0)`. On an EL1 host it still read DAIF. xnu.ko enters XNU with every interrupt masked
(`msr daifset, #0xf`) and nothing hosted clears the mask (ml_set_interrupts_enabled only flips the virtual flag), so
the test `state & DAIF_IRQF` was always true and the function returned: on an EL1 host enable_preemption() never
preempted. The thread kept its CPU until it next turned XNU's interrupts back on with preemption on, blocked, or went
back to user space, and the thread that should have run (a real-time thread, a thread_call) waited that long.

The hosted branch is now taken on every host. It is the code a pure EL0 host has run all along; where the hosted
kernel delivers its timer and IPIs (ml_set_interrupts_enabled, linux64_cpu_idle, linux64_spin_relax, the EL0 IRQ
path) the virtual flag is off, and the branch returns before anything.
---
--- a/osfmk/arm/preemption_disable.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/preemption_disable.c
@@ -69,7 +69,14 @@ kernel_preempt_check(void)
 {
 	uint64_t state;
 
-	if (linux64_el0) {
+	/*
+	 * LINUX64 (xnulinux): hosted, XNU's interrupt state is the virtual flag (ml_set_interrupts_enabled), on an EL1 host
+	 * as on a pure EL0 one. The hardware's mask, which the test below reads, stays set for as long as XNU's kernel runs
+	 * on an EL1 host: with it an urgent AST was never taken here, and a preemption asked for while preemption was off
+	 * (a wakeup under a spinlock, the timer run from where interrupts are re-enabled) waited for the next place
+	 * interrupts are turned back on with preemption on, or for the return to user space.
+	 */
+	if (linux64_hook) {
 		if (!ml_get_interrupts_enabled()) {
 			return;
 		}
